\mt1 Titus
\c 1
\s1 Paul’s Greeting to Titus
\r (\ref 2 Corinthians 8:16–24|2CO 8:16-24\ref*)
\p \v 1 Paul, a servant of God and an apostle of Jesus Christ for \add the\add* faith of God’s elect and \add their\add* knowledge of \add the\add* truth that leads to godliness, \v 2 in \add the\add* hope of eternal life, which God, who cannot lie, promised before time began.\f + \fr 1:2 \ft Literally \fqa before times eternal\f* \v 3 \add In His\add* own time He has made His word \add evident\add* in \add the\add* proclamation entrusted to me by \add the\add* command of God our Savior.
\p \v 4 To Titus, \add my\add* true child in \add our\add* common faith:
\p Grace and peace from God \add the\add* Father and Christ Jesus our Savior.
\s1 Appointing Elders on Crete
\r (\ref 1 Timothy 3:1–7|1TI 3:1-7\ref*; \ref 1 Peter 5:1–4|1PE 5:1-4\ref*)
\p \v 5 The reason I left you in Crete \add was\add* that you would set in order what was unfinished and appoint elders in every town, as I directed you. \v 6 \add An elder must\add* be blameless, \add the\add* husband of but one wife,\f + \fr 1:6 \ft Or \fqa faithful to his wife\f* having children who are believers \add and who are\add* not open to accusation of indiscretion or insubordination.
\p \v 7 As God’s steward, an overseer must be above reproach — not self-willed, not quick-tempered, not given to drunkenness, not violent, not greedy for money. \v 8 Instead, \add he must be\add* hospitable, a lover of good, self-controlled, upright, holy, \add and\add* disciplined. \v 9 He must hold firmly to the faithful word as it was taught, so that he can encourage \add others\add* by sound teaching and refute those who contradict \add it\add*.
\s1 Correcting False Teachers
\r (\ref 1 Timothy 1:3–11|1TI 1:3-11\ref*)
\p \v 10 For many are rebellious and full of empty talk and deception, especially those of the circumcision, \v 11 who must be silenced. For \add the\add* sake of dishonorable gain, \add they\add* undermine entire households \add and\add* teach things they should not. \v 12 \add As\add* one of their own prophets has said, “Cretans \add are\add* always liars, evil beasts, lazy gluttons.”\f + \fr 1:12 \ft This quote, also known as the Epimenides paradox, has been attributed to the Cretan philosopher Epimenides of Knossos.\f*
\p \v 13 This testimony is true. Therefore rebuke them sternly, so that they will be sound in the faith \v 14 \add and\add* will pay no attention to Jewish myths \add or to the\add* commands of men who have rejected the truth.
\p \v 15 To the pure, all things \add are\add* pure; but to the defiled and unbelieving, nothing \add is\add* pure. Indeed, both their minds and \add their\add* consciences are defiled. \v 16 They profess to know God, but by \add their\add* actions they deny \add Him\add*. They are detestable, disobedient, and unfit for any good deed.
\c 2
\s1 Teaching Sound Doctrine
\p \v 1 But \add as for\add* you, speak the things that are consistent with sound doctrine.
\p \v 2 Older \add men\add* are to be temperate, dignified, self-controlled, \add and\add* sound in faith, love, and perseverance.
\p \v 3 Older \add women\add*, likewise, are to be reverent in \add their\add* behavior, not slanderers \add or\add* addicted to much wine, \add but\add* teachers of good. \v 4 In this way they can train the young \add women\add* to love \add their\add* husbands \add and\add* children, \v 5 \add to be\add* self-controlled, pure, managers of their households, kind, \add and\add* submissive to \add their\add* own husbands, so that the word of God will not be discredited.
\p \v 6 In the same way, urge the younger \add men\add* to be self-controlled.
\p \v 7 In everything, show yourself \add to be\add* an example \add by doing\add* good works. In \add your\add* teaching \add show\add* integrity, dignity, \v 8 \add and\add* wholesome speech \add that is\add* above reproach, so that anyone who opposes \add us\add* will be ashamed, having nothing bad to say about us.
\p \v 9 Slaves are to submit to their own masters in everything, to be well-pleasing, not argumentative, \v 10 not stealing \add from them\add*, but showing all good faith, so that in every respect they will adorn the teaching about God our Savior.
\s1 God’s Grace Brings Salvation
\p \v 11 For the grace of God has appeared, bringing salvation to everyone. \v 12 It instructs us to renounce ungodliness and worldly passions, \add and\add* to live sensible, upright, and godly \add lives\add* in the present age, \v 13 as we await the blessed hope and glorious appearance of our great God and Savior Jesus Christ. \v 14 He gave Himself for us to redeem us from all lawlessness and to purify for Himself a people for His own possession, zealous for good deeds.
\p \v 15 Speak these things \add as\add* you encourage and rebuke with all authority. \add Let\add* no one despise you.
\c 3
\s1 Heirs of Grace
\p \v 1 Remind \add the believers\add* to submit to rulers \add and\add* authorities, to be obedient \add and\add* ready for every good work, \v 2 to malign no one, \add and\add* to be peaceable \add and\add* gentle, showing full consideration to everyone.
\p \v 3 For at one time we too were foolish, disobedient, misled, \add and\add* enslaved to all sorts of desires and pleasures — living in malice and envy, being hated \add and\add* hating one another.
\p \v 4 But when the kindness of God our Savior and \add His\add* love for mankind appeared, \v 5 He saved us, not by \add the\add* righteous deeds we had done, but according to His mercy, through \add the\add* washing of new birth\f + \fr 3:5 \ft Or \fqa of regeneration\f* and renewal by \add the\add* Holy Spirit. \v 6  \add This is the Spirit \add* He poured out on us abundantly through Jesus Christ our Savior, \v 7 so that, having been justified by \add His\add* grace, we would become heirs with \add the\add* hope of eternal life. \v 8 \add This\add* saying \add is\add* trustworthy. And I want you to emphasize these things, so that those who have believed God will take care to devote themselves to good deeds. These things are excellent and profitable for the people.
\s1 Avoid Divisions
\r (\ref Romans 16:17–20|ROM 16:17-20\ref*)
\p \v 9 But avoid foolish controversies, genealogies, arguments, and quarrels about \add the\add* law, because \add these things\add* are pointless and worthless.
\p \v 10 Reject a divisive man after a first and second admonition, \v 11 knowing that such a man is corrupt and sinful; he is self-condemned.
\s1 Final Remarks and Greetings
\p \v 12 As soon as I send Artemas or Tychicus to you, make every effort to come to me at Nicopolis, because I have decided to winter there. \v 13 Do your best to equip Zenas the lawyer and Apollos, so that they will have everything they need. \v 14 And our \add people\add* must also learn to devote themselves to good works in order to meet \add the\add* pressing needs \add of others\add*, so that they will not be unfruitful.
\p \v 15 All who \add are\add* with me send you greetings.
\p Greet those who love us in \add the\add* faith.
\p Grace \add be\add* with all of you.\f + \fr 3:15 \ft BYZ and TR include \fqa Amen.\f*
